    fullDescription: "The Hundertwasserhaus is Vienna's most joyfully eccentric building, a municipal apartment block designed by the visionary artist and ecologist Friedensreich Hundertwasser and completed in 1985 as a deliberate rebellion against the 'tyranny of the straight line'. Every conventional rule of architecture is playfully broken: the façade is a patchwork of clashing colours and irregular hand-painted tiles, no two windows are alike, the floors inside undulate ('an uneven floor is a melody to the feet', he said), golden onion domes sprout from the roofline, and more than 250 trees and shrubs grow from terraces and windows, so that greenery cascades down the walls. Hundertwasser refused any fee, wanting only that something ugly not be built on the site, and the result is a beloved, still-inhabited council house of some fifty flats. Because it is a private residence, you cannot go inside, but the exterior is free to admire and endlessly photogenic (do respect the residents and stay on the pavement). Directly opposite, the small Hundertwasser Village, a former tyre workshop reimagined in his style, houses quirky shops and a café, while a few minutes' walk away the Kunst Haus Wien, also his design, is a full museum of his art and hosts photography exhibitions. Together they make an easy, offbeat half-hour or half-day away from the imperial centre, reached by a short tram ride.",
