    fullDescription: "Figlmüller is a Viennese institution and, for many visitors, the definitive place to eat the city's signature dish: the Wiener schnitzel. Family-run since 1905, its original home in a quiet passage off Wollzeile (there is a second, larger branch nearby on Bäckerstraße) has built its fame on one thing done superbly — a schnitzel so enormous it famously hangs over the rim of the plate, pounded paper-thin, breaded in a light, dry, golden crumb that shatters at the fork and fried in a closely guarded blend so it stays crisp and never greasy. Purists note that Figlmüller's version is traditionally made with pork rather than the pricier veal (both are offered), and it comes classically with a wedge of lemon and a side of the tangy Erdäpfel-Vogerlsalat, warm potato and lamb's-lettuce salad. The rest of the menu rounds out the Viennese canon — goulash, Tafelspitz, strudel — but almost everyone is here for the same thing, and the buzzing, wood-panelled rooms fill with a happy mix of locals and travellers. It is neither cheap-cheap nor fine dining, but honest, generous and reliable, and the portions mean you rarely need a starter. Because it is so popular and central, book ahead or come slightly off-peak, and arrive hungry — this is a plate to be reckoned with, and a rite of passage in Vienna.",
