    fullDescription: "Naschmarkt Deli is the most famous of the many eateries lining Vienna's great Naschmarkt, and a brilliant way to soak up the market's cosmopolitan energy over a leisurely meal. Set among the produce stalls near the Kettenbrückengasse end, it is a permanently buzzing, slightly ramshackle stall-restaurant with tables spilling into the market aisle, and it trades on a menu that captures the modern, multicultural flavour of the city: hearty all-day breakfasts and brunches (a big draw, especially at weekends), Austrian staples, plus a strong current of Middle Eastern and Mediterranean dishes — mezze, falafel, shakshuka, grilled meats and fresh juices — reflecting the traders and communities that give the market its character. The vibe is casual, loud and fun rather than refined, the portions are generous and prices moderate, and half the pleasure is people-watching over a coffee or a beer as the market bustles around you. It does not take bookings and is genuinely popular, so it can get crowded and the wait for a table lengthy at prime brunch and lunch times — aim to arrive when it opens, around midday, or in the evening, to snag a seat more easily. Service can be brisk and a touch chaotic when it is heaving, which is part of the market experience. Combine a meal here with a graze along the Naschmarkt stalls and, on a Saturday, a rummage through the neighbouring flea market for a perfect, flavour-packed Viennese half-day.",
