    fullDescription: "One of the most delightful and distinctive things about Vienna is that it is the only major world capital with significant vineyards inside its city limits, and the Heuriger — the traditional wine tavern — is where that heritage comes alive. On the wooded northern edge of the city, villages like Grinzing, Nussdorf, Neustift and Heiligenstadt sit among sloping vineyards, and their Heurigen are rustic, convivial taverns where the local winemakers serve their own young wine (the word 'Heuriger' means 'this year's') straight from the barrel, traditionally marked as open by a bunch of pine or holly twigs (a 'Buschen') hung above the door. The ritual is wonderfully simple and inexpensive: you order the young white wine by the glass or the eighth-of-a-litre, often the fresh, gently fizzy new wine, or in autumn the cloudy, sweet, fermenting 'Sturm', and pair it with a self-service cold buffet of Austrian tavern food — roast pork, cold cuts, cheeses, spreads, bread and pickles — eaten at long communal wooden tables in a cosy panelled room or a leafy garden courtyard. Many taverns lay on live 'Schrammelmusik', the nostalgic accordion-and-violin folk music of old Vienna, adding to the merry, singalong atmosphere as the evening wears on. Beethoven and generations of Viennese have made merry here. Grinzing is the most famous and accessible spot, reached in about half an hour on tram 38, though it can be touristy; venture to Nussdorf or Stammersdorf for a more local feel. A Heuriger evening, ideally combined with a walk through the vineyards to a viewpoint like Kahlenberg, is an essential, joyful and affordable Viennese experience.",
