    fullDescription: "The Augustiner Keller is one of Vienna's great traditional cellars, a vast, atmospheric vaulted space burrowed beneath the Albertina and the Hofburg complex that has been serving wine since 1327 and remains a wonderfully convivial, boisterous place to eat, drink and soak up old-world Viennese cheer. Descending into its long, arched brick tunnels, you find rows of wooden tables filling with a happy, noisy mix of locals and visitors, waiters in aprons ferrying carafes of Austrian wine and foaming mugs of beer, and the warm, informal energy of a place built for merrymaking. The food is hearty, generous and classically Austrian: the house speciality is Stelze, a huge crispy roast pork knuckle, alongside schnitzel, roast meats, goulash, spare ribs and all the traditional trimmings, designed to line the stomach for a long evening of drinking. Prices are reasonable for such a central, historic spot. The real magic, though, comes in the evenings, when live traditional 'Schrammelmusik' — the nostalgic accordion, violin and guitar folk music of old Vienna — strikes up and works its way between the tables, encouraging singing, clinking glasses and a genuinely festive, communal atmosphere that can carry on late into the night. It is touristy in the best sense, a big, warm, fun night out that captures the beer-and-wine-hall side of the city's soul, just steps from the Opera and the Hofburg. Come with a group and an appetite, order the Stelze and a carafe of wine, and settle in for a lively, quintessentially Viennese evening underground.",
