    fullDescription: "Mariahilfer Strasse — 'Mahü' to locals — is Vienna's longest and most popular shopping street, a lively 1.8-kilometre artery running from near the MuseumsQuartier and the Ring out to the Westbahnhof, and it is where ordinary Viennese do much of their everyday shopping. This is the city's high-street heartland, a democratic and energetic contrast to the luxury of the inner-city lanes: it is packed with the big international fashion and lifestyle chains (H&M, Zara, Uniqlo, Mango and the like), electronics and homeware stores, several department stores, bookshops, cafés, restaurants and the flagship branches of many popular brands, offering plentiful choice at affordable to mid-range prices. Much of the street has been remodelled into an attractive, largely traffic-calmed shared zone with wide pavements, seating, trees and space to stroll, making it pleasant to wander even when busy, and its length means you can shop for hours. Beyond the shopping, it is a good window onto contemporary, everyday Vienna and gives easy access to the appealing neighbourhoods on either side — the boutique-and-café-filled Neubau and Spittelberg to the north, Mariahilf and the Naschmarkt to the south. Because it is so long, it helps to use the U3 metro, which runs beneath it with several stations, to hop between sections. As with most of Austria, shops generally close on Sundays, so plan weekday or Saturday visits. For high-street and mid-range shopping with the widest choice, a relaxed pedestrian atmosphere and a taste of local life away from the imperial core, Mariahilfer Strasse is the place, and an easy walk or ride from the centre.",
