    fullDescription: "For a completely different shopping experience from Vienna's polished streets, the Naschmarkt is the city's largest and liveliest market, a 1.5-kilometre strip of stalls that is as much a shopping destination as an eating one, and a superb place to pick up edible souvenirs and soak up cosmopolitan Viennese life. Along its aisles you can buy beautiful produce, Austrian and Central European delicacies, cheeses and charcuterie, spices, teas, dried fruits and nuts, olives, wines and gourmet treats to take home, from stalls run by traders reflecting the city's diverse communities, with a strong Middle Eastern and Mediterranean presence adding colour and flavour. It is an atmospheric, sensory place to browse, taste and stock up, interspersed with delis and cafés for a break. The market's other, and for bargain-hunters most exciting, dimension appears every Saturday, when its western end near Kettenbrückengasse hosts one of Central Europe's great flea markets: a sprawling, chaotic treasure trove of antiques, vintage clothing, vinyl records, old books, jewellery, furniture, curios and outright junk, spread across scores of stalls and drawing dealers and rummagers from across the city. Haggling is expected, the atmosphere is fun and the potential finds genuine, but the early bird wins, so serious hunters arrive not long after dawn. Free to browse and endlessly photogenic, the Naschmarkt combines food shopping, souvenir hunting, people-watching and, on Saturdays, flea-market bargaining into one of the most enjoyable and authentic shopping experiences in Vienna. Combine it with lunch at one of the market restaurants for a perfect half-day.",
