    fullDescription: "Bratislava, the capital of neighbouring Slovakia, is the easiest, closest and cheapest international day trip from Vienna, sitting just an hour east down the Danube and making a fun, relaxed contrast to the grandeur of the Austrian capital. Direct trains run frequently and cheaply from Vienna's main station, and in summer you can even arrive in style aboard the Twin City Liner catamaran that speeds along the Danube between the two cities. Bratislava is a manageable, human-scale city easily explored on foot in a day. Its charming, compact old town is a pleasant maze of pastel Baroque and Rococo houses, pedestrian lanes, lively café and restaurant terraces, and pretty squares centred on the old town hall, dotted with the city's famous quirky bronze statues — most beloved being 'Čumil', a workman peeking out of a manhole. Above the town rises the boxy, four-towered Bratislava Castle, rebuilt in gleaming white, whose ramparts give fine views over the red rooftops, the sprawling Danube and, on a clear day, three countries. Other sights include St Martin's Cathedral, the blue Art Nouveau church of St Elizabeth, and the quirky UFO observation deck on the SNP bridge. A real draw for many is value: food, drink and coffee are noticeably cheaper than in Vienna, making it a great spot for an affordable lunch or dinner of hearty Slovak fare and beer. Bratislava will not overwhelm you with must-see monuments, but its easygoing charm, walkability, castle views, quirky character and bargain prices make it a genuinely enjoyable and effortless day out across the border from Vienna.",
