    fullDescription: "Stift Melk, the great Benedictine abbey of Melk, is one of the most magnificent Baroque buildings in the world and a spectacular day trip from Vienna, standing in golden-yellow splendour on a rocky bluff high above the Danube at the western gateway to the Wachau Valley. Founded in 1089 and rebuilt in its current glorious form in the early eighteenth century, the abbey is a masterpiece of Austrian Baroque, and a guided or self-guided tour of its state rooms reveals treasure after treasure. The showstoppers are the abbey church, a riot of gilded, frescoed, marbled Baroque exuberance beneath a soaring dome, and above all the breathtaking library, a vast hall lined floor to ceiling with tens of thousands of ancient leather-bound volumes beneath a magnificent ceiling fresco, one of the most beautiful monastic libraries anywhere and a genuine 'wow' moment. The Marble Hall, with its illusionistic painted ceiling, and the long terrace and balcony linking it to the church, which offers sweeping views over the river, the town and the surrounding countryside, complete the experience, along with a museum tracing the monastery's thousand-year history and pleasant terraced gardens. The little town of Melk at the foot of the abbey is charming for a stroll and lunch. Reached in about an hour by direct train from Vienna, Melk works beautifully as a half-day in its own right or, ideally, as one end of a full-day Wachau Valley trip combined with a Danube river cruise to Dürnstein and Krems. For lovers of architecture, history and sheer Baroque grandeur, Melk Abbey is unmissable.",
