    fullDescription: "Hallstatt is the most photographed village in Austria and one of the most beautiful in the world, a tiny, storybook cluster of pastel houses, flower-decked balconies and a slender church spire wedged impossibly onto a narrow ledge between a glassy Alpine lake and steep, forested mountains in the Salzkammergut lake district. Its fame is now global — it has even been copied wholesale as a replica town in China — and the classic view of the village reflected in the still water, framed by peaks, is the definitive image of romantic Austria. Beyond simply soaking up the scenery, wandering the car-free lanes, and taking a boat or ferry on the lake, there is real substance: Hallstatt has been mined for salt for some seven thousand years, giving its name to an entire era of prehistory (the Hallstatt culture), and the ancient salt mine above the village, reached by funicular, offers tours through the underground workings along with the dramatic 'Skywalk' viewing platform overlooking the lake and village far below. It is genuinely lovely, but two caveats matter. First, it is very popular and can feel overwhelmed by day-trippers in peak season, so early morning or an overnight stay rewards you with the magical, quiet version. Second, and importantly for planning, it is a long way from Vienna — around three and a half to four hours each way by train (with a change and a scenic ferry across the lake to reach the village), which makes it a very long day trip. Many travellers prefer to stay a night, or to visit as part of a wider tour of the lakes and Salzburg. If you do go for the day, start at dawn, book train tickets ahead, and be prepared for a lot of travel — but the reward is one of the most breathtaking spots in the Alps.",
