    fullDescription: "The Semmering Railway offers one of the most rewarding and unusual day trips from Vienna, combining a ride on a genuine engineering marvel with easy access to beautiful Alpine scenery and walking, all within about ninety minutes of the city. Opened in 1854, the Semmering was the first true mountain railway in the world to be built across high mountains, an astonishing feat of nineteenth-century engineering that climbs over the Semmering pass through a remarkable series of curved tunnels, towering multi-arched stone viaducts and cuttings carved into steep, wooded slopes, and it was recognised as a UNESCO World Heritage Site for its pioneering fusion of technology and landscape. Simply riding the regular scheduled train over the pass is a scenic delight in itself, the carriage winding slowly across the dramatic viaducts with sweeping views over forested valleys and mountains, but the trip becomes really special if you get off and explore. The area around Semmering and neighbouring Payerbach-Reichenau was a fashionable Alpine resort in the imperial era, dotted with grand villas and Belle Époque hotels, and it offers excellent, well-marked walking. The signature route is the Bahnwanderweg, a scenic trail that follows the line and lets you walk beneath and alongside the famous viaducts and tunnels, admiring the engineering from the outside and enjoying panoramic viewpoints, while other trails lead higher into the hills and to the Sonnwendstein peak. In winter there is modest skiing and snow walking. Fresh air, mountain scenery, a fascinating piece of railway heritage and good hiking, all reached by a straightforward and inexpensive direct train, make the Semmering a superb and refreshing escape into the Alps from Vienna, ideal for a fine-weather day.",
