    fullDescription: "Vienna's U-Bahn is one of the best metro systems in Europe — clean, safe, punctual, frequent and wonderfully easy to use — and it is the backbone of getting around the city. There are five lines (numbered U1, U2, U3, U4 and U6; there is no U5 yet, though one is under construction), colour-coded and well signposted in English as well as German, and between them they cover the whole city efficiently, with useful interchanges at hubs like Karlsplatz, Stephansplatz, Schwedenplatz and Westbahnhof. Trains run roughly every two to five minutes at peak times and every five to ten off-peak, from around 5am to midnight daily, and — a great bonus for visitors — the metro runs all night long on Fridays, Saturdays and the nights before public holidays, making late nights out easy. Ticketing is refreshingly simple and integrated across the metro, trams, buses and S-Bahn within the core zone: a single ticket costs €2.40 and lets you travel in one direction with any changes, while for visitors the time-based passes are far better value — 24-hour (€8), 48-hour (€14.10) and 72-hour (€17.10) passes give unlimited travel, and the excellent-value weekly ticket (€17.10, valid Monday to the following Monday) can be cheaper still if your stay aligns. The Vienna City Card and the various tourist passes also bundle transport. Crucially, you must buy and, for single/print tickets, validate your ticket before boarding (at machines, in the app, or from tobacconists), as the system is honour-based with roving inspectors and steep fines for fare-dodging. Download the WienMobil or an app like Google Maps for real-time routing. Fast, comprehensive and easy, the U-Bahn combined with the trams will get you almost anywhere you want to go.",
