    fullDescription: "Complementing the metro, Vienna has one of the largest and most charming tram networks in the world, along with an extensive bus system, all run by Wiener Linien and using exactly the same integrated tickets as the U-Bahn, so a single fare or a day/weekly pass covers seamless travel across all of them. The trams (Straßenbahn, or 'Bim' to locals) are a delight and often the nicest way to get around the central districts, rattling along the surface past the great sights, and for visitors they double as an unbeatable-value sightseeing tool: riding the trams that loop the majestic Ringstrasse — lines 1, 2, D and 71 between them trace the grand boulevard — takes you slowly past the State Opera, the museums, the Parliament, the neo-Gothic City Hall (Rathaus), the Burgtheater, the university and the Votivkirche, effectively giving you a scenic circuit of imperial Vienna for the price of a normal ticket rather than an expensive tourist bus. Other useful lines include tram 38 out to the Heuriger wine villages of Grinzing. Buses fill in the gaps the rails do not reach, including the narrow inner-city streets (served by small 'City Bus' minibuses) and the routes up into the Vienna Woods and hills; there are also night buses (NightLine) covering the hours when trams and most metros stop on weeknights, while the metro itself runs all night at weekends. As with the metro, you must have a valid ticket before boarding and validate single tickets, with fines for those caught without. Real-time apps make navigation easy. Together, the trams and buses extend the reach of Vienna's superb public transport to every corner, and the Ring trams in particular are a scenic highlight in their own right.",
