    fullDescription: "Understanding Vienna's ticket options helps you travel cheaply and avoid fines, and happily the system is simple and integrated: one ticket type covers the metro, trams, buses and city-zone S-Bahn. For occasional journeys, a single ticket costs €2.40 and allows one continuous trip in one direction with any changes. For most visitors, though, a time-based pass is much better value and more convenient: the 24-hour (€8), 48-hour (€14.10) and 72-hour (€17.10) passes give unlimited travel and pay for themselves in just a few rides, while the standout bargain is the weekly ticket (€17.10), valid Monday morning to the following Monday, which can be the cheapest option of all if your trip fits the calendar week. Beyond plain transport, two tourist cards cause confusion, so it pays to know the difference. The Vienna City Card (from around €17) bundles public transport for your chosen duration with a booklet of smaller discounts (a few euros off many museums, cafés, shops and tours) — worthwhile only if you will actually use enough of the discounts to beat a plain transport pass. The separate, pricier Vienna Pass is an 'all-you-can-see' card giving free entry to many attractions plus a hop-on-hop-off bus, which can save money only if you cram in a lot of paid sights each day. For the majority of independent travellers who walk a lot and pick a few key attractions, a straightforward 48/72-hour or weekly Wiener Linien pass is usually the smartest, cheapest choice. Buy tickets from machines at stations, tobacconists (Trafik), online or in the WienMobil app; remember that single and printed tickets must be validated in the blue machines before you travel, as inspectors are frequent and fines steep.",
