    fullDescription: "Vienna is a city where you can very happily get by without ever taking a taxi or renting a car, because the public transport is so comprehensive, cheap and pleasant and the centre so walkable, but it is worth knowing your options for late nights, heavy luggage or trips off the network. Official taxis are metered, clean and reliable: fares start at around €3.80 with a per-kilometre rate of roughly €1.40, so short city hops are affordable and a ride from the airport runs about €35–45. You can find them at ranks across the city, phone a radio-taxi company (numbers like 40100 or 31300 are well known), or, most conveniently, summon one through an app. Ride-hailing is well established and often the easiest choice for visitors: Uber, Bolt and the taxi-app FreeNow all operate in Vienna, offering upfront pricing, card payment and no language barrier, with fares that are frequently similar to or cheaper than street taxis. For most sightseeing, though, the U-Bahn, trams and buses will be quicker and far cheaper than any car, and traffic and paid short-stay parking zones (Kurzparkzonen) across the inner districts make driving in the city more hassle than help. Renting a car really only makes sense if you plan to explore the wider countryside — the Wachau Valley, the lakes, or Alpine areas — beyond the reach of the excellent train network; even then, many of the best day trips (Wachau, Melk, Salzburg, Bratislava, Semmering) are easily and enjoyably done by rail. If you do drive, use the park-and-ride facilities at outer metro stations and switch to public transport for the centre. In short: rely on public transport, keep taxis and apps for convenience, and consider a car only for rural excursions.",
