    fullDescription: "Vienna is a year-round city-break destination, and the cost of flying there swings with the seasons, so timing your trip can save a good deal of money. The cheapest months to fly are in the depths of the winter low season — February and November stand out as the best value, with average round-trip fares dipping to around $248, roughly a third to 40% cheaper than the summer peak — with January also cheap once the New Year rush has passed. Prices climb through spring, and the summer months of June, July and August are the most expensive, when warm weather and school holidays drive fares to around $400 return and beyond, with August often the priciest. The shoulder seasons of April–May and September–October offer a sweet spot of pleasant weather, thinner crowds and moderate prices. One important exception to the winter bargain rule is the Christmas-market and New Year period: from late November through December, Vienna's famous Advent markets make it hugely popular, pushing December flight and hotel prices up sharply, and the days around New Year (when the city hosts its famous concerts and balls) are dearest of all. To get the best fares whenever you go, book around five to seven weeks in advance, be flexible with your dates, and fly midweek (Tuesday and Wednesday departures are typically cheaper than weekends, as Vienna is a popular weekend-break city). Austrian Airlines, the flag carrier hubbed in Vienna, offers wide connectivity and periodic sales, while low-cost carriers serve the city from many European cities. Setting a price alert and comparing across the search tool lets you pounce when fares drop. In short: for the cheapest flights, target February or November, avoid the Christmas and summer peaks, book a month or two ahead and choose midweek dates.",
