    fullDescription: "Vienna is a fairly expensive Western European capital but not an extreme one — comparable to or a little cheaper than Paris, London or the Nordic cities, and more expensive than neighbouring Prague, Budapest or Bratislava — and how much you spend depends heavily on your style. A budget backpacker can get by on roughly €60–80 a day: a hostel dorm bed (around €25–40), self-catering or cheap eats like a sausage stand, a slice of pizza, a Naschmarkt snack or a giant budget schnitzel (€5–13), a transport pass, and a diet of the city's many free sights — its churches, parks, markets, the Ring, the Belvedere gardens, and standing-room opera tickets from €13 — keeps costs down. A comfortable mid-range traveller should budget around €120–200 a day per person including a decent three-star or boutique hotel room (shared, so €60–120 each), sit-down restaurant meals and coffee-house treats (€15–35 per meal), public transport and a couple of paid attractions or a concert. Luxury travellers staying in the grand palace hotels and dining in fine restaurants will spend €350 a day and up with ease. Beyond accommodation, the main costs are food and sightseeing, and both offer easy savings: eat your main meal at lunch when many restaurants offer cheaper set menus, graze the markets, picnic in the parks, take advantage of the numerous free or cheap attractions, buy a transport pass rather than singles, and use standing-room tickets for the opera and cheaper gallery-tier seats for concerts. Coffee-house culture, while a lovely indulgence at €5–7 a cup, is one where you pay for the right to linger for hours, which is good value in its way. With a little planning, Vienna can be enjoyed richly across a wide range of budgets.",
