    fullDescription: "Vienna's world-class palaces and museums are a major draw but individual admission adds up, so it helps to know the costs and the ways to save. The headline sights sit broadly in the €15–31 range: Schönbrunn Palace costs around €27 for the Grand Tour (less for the shorter Imperial Tour), the Belvedere with Klimt's 'The Kiss' is €17.50, the Kunsthistorisches Museum €21, the Natural History Museum €16, the Hofburg's Sisi Ticket (Imperial Apartments, Sisi Museum, Silver Collection) €19.50, the Imperial Treasury €14, the Albertina €16.90, and the Leopold Museum in the MuseumsQuartier €17. Concerts and the opera vary hugely, from €13 standing-room places at the State Opera to €30–120+ for good seats at the Musikverein. If you plan to visit many paid attractions in a short time, the Vienna Pass (an all-inclusive card giving free entry to dozens of sights plus a hop-on-hop-off bus) can save money — but only if you sightsee intensively, so tot up what you actually intend to see first; for a more relaxed itinerary of a few sights, paying individually is usually cheaper. Numerous combined tickets also offer savings, such as the Kunsthistorisches plus Natural History Museum combo. Crucially, a great deal of Vienna is free or cheap: all the churches including Stephansdom's nave, the Belvedere and palace gardens, the parks, the Ring, the Naschmarkt, the Prater park, the atmospheric Central Cemetery and simply wandering the imperial streetscape cost nothing, and under-19s enter many state museums free. Booking the big sights online secures timed entry and skips queues. Balance a few paid highlights with the city's abundant free pleasures and your sightseeing budget will stretch comfortably.",
