    fullDescription: "The Wiener Festwochen (Vienna Festival) is the city's flagship festival of the performing arts and one of the most important cultural events in the Viennese calendar, a five-week programme running from around mid-May to mid or late June that fills theatres, concert halls and unconventional venues across the city with ambitious, international and often boundary-pushing work. Founded in 1951, the festival has a strongly contemporary, avant-garde and intellectually adventurous character, distinguishing it from Vienna's more traditional classical offerings: its rich programme spans cutting-edge theatre and directors' work, contemporary and experimental dance, new music and opera, performance art, discussions and projects that frequently engage with pressing social and political themes, drawing leading and emerging artists, companies and directors from Austria and around the world. It gives the late-spring city a vibrant, forward-looking cultural energy and offers visitors a chance to experience Vienna not just as a museum of imperial and classical heritage but as a living, questioning contemporary arts capital. The festival traditionally opens with a large, free public event and concert on Rathausplatz in front of the City Hall, a popular celebration that launches the weeks of programming and is open to all. Individual performances are ticketed, with prices varying widely by event and venue, and the programme, announced in advance, ranges from accessible crowd-pleasers to challenging experimental pieces, so it rewards a look at the schedule to find what appeals. For culturally curious travellers visiting in late spring — itself one of the best times to come for weather and atmosphere — the Wiener Festwochen adds a stimulating extra dimension, and the free opening festivities are a lively, welcoming way to sample the city's contemporary creative scene.",
